{
  // ⚠️ The app gets its OWN tsconfig, and the root one is left exactly as it was.
  //
  // The root `tsconfig.json` is `"module": "NodeNext"` / `"moduleResolution": "NodeNext"`, and every
  // import across `src/` and `scripts/` is written with an explicit `.js` extension because nodenext
  // requires it — `import { runLoop } from '../src/agent/loop.js'`. Letting Next rewrite the root
  // config would silently stop ~33 scripts and the whole report pipeline from resolving under tsx.
  //
  // ⚠️ **This file is `nodenext` too, as of Unit 6, and that is not cosmetic.** Next's defaults are
  // `esnext` + `bundler`; under those, **Turbopack cannot resolve `src/`'s `.js` specifiers** — it
  // does no `.js` → `.ts` extension aliasing, so `app/` importing the store died inside
  // `src/store/reports.ts` on its own `import { pooled } from './db.js'`. `experimental.extensionAlias`
  // does not fix it: that option is read only by webpack, and Turbopack accepts it and ignores it.
  //
  // ⚠️ **The cost, so the next person recognises it.** nodenext makes TypeScript model Node ESM while
  // Turbopack keeps bundler interop, and the two disagree at **a default import of a CommonJS
  // module**. `next` ships no `exports` map, so `import Link from 'next/link.js'` binds
  // `module.exports` rather than the component. Named imports are fine — `next/navigation.js` and
  // `next/server.js` both pass. That is why `app/` uses a plain `<a href>` and not `next/link`.
  //
  // `next.config.ts` points Next here with `typescript.tsconfigPath`, so Next reads and rewrites
  // THIS file and never touches the root. Verified: `next build` typechecks the app against these
  // settings while `tsc -p tsconfig.json --noEmit` still passes over src/ and scripts/ unchanged.
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2022",
    "lib": [
      "dom",
      "dom.iterable",
      "esnext"
    ],
    "module": "nodenext",
    "moduleResolution": "nodenext",
    "jsx": "react-jsx",
    "allowJs": true,
    "strict": true,
    "noEmit": true,
    "esModuleInterop": true,
    "resolveJsonModule": true,
    "isolatedModules": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "incremental": true,
    "plugins": [
      {
        "name": "next"
      }
    ]
  },
  // ⚠️ `src` and `scripts` are excluded as ENTRY POINTS, which is not the same as unchecked. They are
  // the root tsconfig's job, and listing them here would drag all ~33 scripts into every `next build`.
  // The `src/` files `app/` actually imports are still typechecked, transitively — `exclude` filters
  // `include`, it does not stop an imported file from entering the program. Since Unit 6 that is the
  // store and `agent/narrate.ts`, and they check clean because this file is now nodenext like theirs.
  "include": [
    "next-env.d.ts",
    "next.config.ts",
    "app/**/*.ts",
    "app/**/*.tsx",
    ".next/types/**/*.ts",
    ".next/dev/types/**/*.ts"
  ],
  "exclude": [
    "node_modules",
    "src",
    "scripts"
  ]
}
